Two things I found before the first round:

- **There is no glossary file in this workspace.** `/workspace` has no `GLOSSARY.md`, no `docs/adr/`, and no checkout code (`src/index.js` is an empty export), so I'm working from your quoted definition and can't cross-check against code.
- **The conflict is real.** Your glossary says an Order is what the customer *submits* at checkout, and your team uses "order" for something that was *never submitted*. By your own definition that thing is not an Order, so one word is covering two concepts with different lifecycles.

I haven't written anything yet; I'll write `GLOSSARY.md` as each term is settled. Round 1 covers everything I can ask without guessing at your answers.

---

❓ **Q1 - Which meaning of "Order" wins**: Should the glossary definition stand, so that an Order only exists once the customer has submitted, and the unsubmitted thing is always a **Cart**? "Order" for a cart, along with "draft order", "pending order" and "open order", would go on the Avoid list, and "abandoned order" becomes "abandoned cart".

➡️ Yes. Submission is the point where the customer commits and where prices, stock and payment start to matter, so the word should change there too. Widening Order to include carts would force everyone to say "submitted order" forever.

---

❓ **Q2 - Is there a third concept between Cart and Order**: Take two scenarios:

- (a) Dana adds two items and closes the tab.
- (b) Dana adds two items, clicks "Checkout", enters a shipping address, picks a delivery option, then closes the tab.

I suspect your team mostly says "order" for (b), because it has an address and a shipping choice and so looks like an order. Should (b) be a distinct concept with its own name, a **Checkout**: one customer's in-progress attempt to turn a Cart into an Order?

➡️ Yes. The lifecycle would be Cart → Checkout → Order. If (a) and (b) are both just "Cart", the address and delivery data has no home and people will keep reaching for "order" to describe it.

---

❓ **Q3 - Is the loose usage only in speech**: I can't check this from here. Is "order" for unsubmitted carts only how people talk, with no table, status value (such as `order.status = 'draft'`), API or dashboard ("abandoned orders") that stores or counts unsubmitted carts as orders?

➡️ I'm assuming yes, speech only, until you say otherwise. If it is baked into a schema or a report, fixing the term also means a migration and a change to reported numbers, and that becomes its own branch of the design.

---

❓ **Q4 - Where the redesign starts and stops**: Should the redesign cover the span from "customer starts checkout on a Cart" to "an Order exists and the customer has been told so"? Cart-building (browsing, adding items) would sit upstream and out of scope. Fulfilment, refunds and returns would sit downstream and out of scope, apart from the handoff.

➡️ Yes. That span is where the Cart/Order confusion lives, and it is small enough to design fully.

---

❓ **Q5 - What kind of redesign this is**: Is this primarily a redesign of the model and flow (which states exist, what moves a Cart to an Order, what happens on failure), with screens following from that, rather than a visual refresh of an unchanged flow?

➡️ Yes. A team that can't agree on what an Order is usually has an unclear transition underneath. If it is really a UI refresh, tell me what is driving it (conversion, new payment methods, tech debt), because that changes most of the later questions.

---

❓ **Q6 - Where the glossary lives**: Should I create `GLOSSARY.md` at the root of this workspace as the single glossary for checkout, seeded with your existing Order definition once Q1 is settled? If the real glossary lives elsewhere, point me to it and I'll read it for other terms that touch checkout.

➡️ Yes, create it here. A glossary that isn't next to the code tends not to be followed, which may be part of how the two meanings drifted apart.

