Your glossary and your team disagree: under the glossary, an unsubmitted cart cannot be an order. Anything built on the word (order counts, order IDs, "abandoned orders") will mean different things to different people until one usage is dropped.

I checked `/workspace`: there is no `GLOSSARY.md` and no checkout code, only an empty stub. The glossary line you quoted is all I have to work from, and there is no code to cross-check.

Here is the first round of questions; answering "yes" accepts my recommendation.

❓ **Q1 - One meaning for Order**: Should **Order** keep the glossary meaning (it exists only once the customer submits) and the unsubmitted thing always be called a **Cart**? The alternative is to stretch Order to cover both with a "draft" status, which means every report, ID and email has to say which kind it means.

➡️ Yes. An Order is submitted; a Cart is not; "order" becomes a term to avoid for carts, and "abandoned order" becomes "abandoned cart".

---

❓ **Q2 - A third concept between Cart and Order**: A customer adds three items, enters a shipping address, picks a delivery option, then closes the tab. Another customer adds three items and never clicks "checkout". Are those different things in your domain? I suspect the first is what the team actually means by "order".

➡️ Yes. Name the in-progress attempt a **Checkout**: it starts when the customer begins checking out a Cart and ends in an Order or in abandonment.

---

❓ **Q3 - What counts as "submitted"**: The customer clicks "Place order" and the card is declined. Does an Order exist? Either it does, with payment outcome as a state of the Order, or it does not until payment succeeds and the customer is still in checkout.

➡️ Yes, the Order exists from the click. That matches your glossary literally and still works for payment methods that confirm later, such as bank transfer or 3-D Secure. The cost is that Orders with failed payment must be excluded from sales figures.

---

❓ **Q4 - Scope of the redesign**: Is the redesign limited to the path from a non-empty Cart to a submitted Order, leaving out how items get into the Cart and what happens after submission (fulfilment, returns, invoicing)?

➡️ Yes. Draw the line there, so we settle terms only for Cart, the checkout attempt, Order and payment.

---

❓ **Q5 - Where the glossary lives**: Should I create `GLOSSARY.md` at the root of this repo and record each term there as we settle it, starting with your existing Order definition? If the real glossary lives somewhere else, tell me where.

➡️ Yes. Keeping it in the repo next to the code makes it the copy developers actually read.

The next round depends on these answers: what an Order copies from the Cart at submission, what happens to the Cart afterwards, and guest versus signed-in customers.

