❓ **Q1** - **One word, one meaning**: "Account" currently means both the company that pays you and each person who signs in. Team plans make these different things with different counts (one payer, many people). Shall we keep **Account** for the payer, as your glossary says, and call the person who signs in a **User**, with "account" banned as a word for a person?

➡️ Yes. The glossary meaning wins and the sign-in code is what changes. "User" is the least surprising word for a person with credentials, where "Member" is better saved for the relationship in Q3.

---

❓ **Q2** - **Is the payer always a company?**: Take a freelancer who pays on their own today and later upgrades to a team plan and invites two colleagues. Shall we say every payer is an Account, even a single person, and that the same Account carries on through the upgrade? That would broaden the glossary definition to "the customer that pays us: a company or an individual".

➡️ Yes. If solo payers are a different kind of thing, every upgrade becomes a migration, and "the company that pays us" is already inaccurate if you have any solo customers.

---

❓ **Q3** - **Can one person belong to more than one Account?**: Take a contractor who works for two of your customers under the same email address. Shall we treat the person as one User with a separate **Membership** in each Account, rather than tying a User to exactly one Account?

➡️ Yes. Model it as many-to-many now, even if the first release only lets a User join one Account. Going from one to many later is the expensive direction, since it touches sessions, permissions and every "current account" lookup.

